## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found 2026-08-31 by lane-b057 while walking a grep list of
`named_types_equivalent` call sites for the B056 follow-up. Pre-existing:
identical on the merge-base and branch binaries, and outside that ticket's
scope. Filed rather than folded in.

## Defect

Binding a function to a funcdef handle does not check the RETURN type:

```angelscript
funcdef void Maker();
int MakeDistinct() { return 1; }

void Use() {
    Maker@ b = MakeDistinct;   // silent — return types differ
}
```

Zero diagnostics. Parameter lists are compared; the return type is not.

## Why it matters

This is a false NEGATIVE — the LSP accepts a binding the game rejects — so it
is the milder direction, but it is squarely in the family this batch has been
closing (B023, B060: what binds to a funcdef slot). It also sits next to code
that was just changed: `funcdef_types_compatible` and
`funcdef_handle_compatible` are two of the four `named_types_equivalent` call
sites B056's follow-up probed, and both were shown reachable.

## Steps to Reproduce

`openplanet-lsp check` on the snippet above.

## Expected Behavior

An error on the assignment, naming the return-type mismatch.

## Actual Behavior

Silent.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)

Red: the snippet above.

Variants, per repo CLAUDE.md — generalize rather than pinning one case:

- matching return type still binds (no regression)
- differing PRIMITIVE return (`int` vs `void`, `uint` vs `float`)
- differing HANDLE return (`CMwNod@` vs `CGameCtnBlock@`)
- handle vs value return of the same type (`T@` vs `T`)
- `const` differences on the return
- differing return WITH matching params, and matching return WITH differing
  params — the second already errors, so it pins that this fix does not
  disturb the existing parameter check
- the same shapes through `startnew(...)` and through a funcdef-typed
  parameter, not only through an assignment
- a workspace funcdef as well as an engine one

**Game probe before treating LSP silence as ground truth** — the game is down
as of 2026-08-30, so this needs a live REJECT to confirm the expected
behaviour. A REJECT is solid evidence; do not infer from an ACCEPT.

